WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
foreign minister good of you to take the  time this afternoon thank you so much  for joining us here on sky news  thank you for having me good evening um  a lot more diplomacy going on this week  western leaders are getting together to  talk about what more they can do to help  ukraine i know that the us secretary of  state anthony blinken visited your  country yesterday and the discussion was  about just that what more can countries  like estonia do what was the outcome of  those discussions  yesterday we had very important  discussions with secretary uh blink and  how the international community could  support the end of the war in ukraine  which was unjustifiedly started by  russia  and of course we must continue to  support ukraine politically economically  continue to give humanitarian aid to  ukraine but also defensive means at the  same time it is very important to  pressure the russian federation to stop  this unjustified and very unfortunate  war which is totally  unacceptable because it is severe  violation of the international law  severe violation of  security related agreements of europe  can you talk to me about  there's  there's a bit of confusion today over  supplying jets to ukraine which they  have asked for and poland thought it had  come up with a solution it wanted to  send jets to germany and then they would  be sent on to ukraine but the united  states says that was untenable  because it would be  perhaps seen as an act of aggression by  the west and nato  towards russia it's a very sensitive  time in what kind of support you do and  do not offer to ukraine what line do you  think there is in what you can offer  them why can't you offer them jets  it is very important to continue provide  continue to provide defensive equipment  to ukraine so that they could protect  their country  and so far we have seen that more than  20 countries already have provided  defensive equipment to uh ukraine for me  it is very difficult to comment the  decisions of other countries but what  can i what i can say that estonia itself  does not fight does not have fighters to  provide but we have provided and donated  this kind of defensive equipment what we  could  deliver and estonia has already  supported ukraine  with  defensive equipment for the about uh 200  million euros it is a big support for  ukraine and we very much welcome that  also other countries have provided  different defensive means for ukraine  which have been possible for them  yeah a lot being made of the fact that  what is being supplied by nato members  and western nations are  defensive weapons um  at what point  is that not enough because president  zielenski every time we hear from him  every time he makes an appeal to western  leaders it's about wanting more what if  those defensive weapons aren't enough  for ukraine  here of course it is a very important to  speak about the main goal the main goal  is to help to end this war and therefore  we the western countries we are trying  to support of course ukraine with  defensive equipment so that they could  defend their country but at the same  time we continue with political pressure  towards russia so that russia would stop  this war which they started and also  would withdraw their military  troops from  ukraine so we are working in two  parallel directions with this regard and  of course i totally understand the  concerns  of president zelinski because what we  see the war is not stopped yet therefore  international community must continue to  work out  opportunities how we can support to end  this war  this war in ukraine will be particularly  alarming for the people of estonia  because estonia is one of the few nato  member states that has direct borders  with russia and when your prime minister  met with the secretary of state anthony  blinken yesterday um she said that um  she is asking to establish a permanent  and meaningful nato forward defense in  the baltic region a permanent defense  because estonia you will be watching  what's going on in ukraine and feeling  particularly vulnerable  yes we have seen  russia's aggressive behavior already for  many years against its neighbors and of  course  this must be reflected also in  documents of nature  in nato  in nato's last year summit it was  jointly agreed that  russia poses threat due to transatlantic  security therefore we started already  last year with these the seasons that we  must continue to strengthen the defense  and endurance posture of nato and from  our perspective yes we need some more  permanent solutions how to defend how to  strengthen the defense posture here in  europe and we believe that we must have  forward defense posture in place  for our region and also we must have  better um air defense systems in place  so we continue to work towards these  solutions within the nato together with  our nato allies  uh foreign minister i know just a a  couple of weeks ago you were in ukraine  you had to cut that visit short so  you've seen the impact of what is  happening on the ground in ukraine but  you have also witnessed over the border  in your country the influx of refugees  perhaps you can talk to us about the  numbers you've received so far  and what's happening when they get to  you what provisions you have from them  because we talk about shelter and the  basics that they need straight away  food and medicine but they also need  jobs they need somewhere to stay because  we don't know how long this is going to  last for what's been the response in  estonia to help them  this is very unfortunate that as of  today we have seen more than 2 million  people already have left ukraine and  they are looking for homes in other  neighboring countries in estonia here we  have accepted by today we have accepted  about 10 000 refugees we have  four refugee centers here in estonia  which register the refugees and then  provide them  places to live most of them  will be  staying with the relatives who were  already here in estonia or friends or  co-workers but if they don't have other  place to stay then we provide hotels for  them for the period of time that there  is another solution found for them of  course at the same time we provide also  uh social security uh and also medical  care because we also see that about the  third of the refugees are children so  they may need different kind of support  and yesterday morning i started my day  also in the refugee center and wanted to  see with my own eyes how this  registration works out and how these  people are helped in estonia and i can  really say that  those people who reach here in estonia  they are warmly welcomed they have been  provided with food with help and  assistance how to organize their life  here in estonia until they can return to  home or transfer to other countries  where they may have relatives of friends  and foreign minister i'll ask you one  final question because this is a hotly  contested topic in the uk at the moment  and  how slowly  the wheels are turning to get ukrainian  refugees a visa to enter the uk just how  quick is the process in estonia how  quickly can people be granted entry into  your country from the time they reach  the border  in estonia ukrainians can enter  visa-free therefore they just  packed into the buses in in mostly in  poland on sometimes in other neighboring  countries and then they are brought to  estonia we have  ngos in place who organize this  transportation and so basically they  don't need any document any documents to  enter estonia but of course here in  estonia we just register them and  provide with  documents also uh phone numbers and  security  uh codes so that they can start their  lives here in estonia

